WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:09.546
Paper may not seem like a significant invention, but it is no exaggeration to say that it has

2
00:00:09.606 --> 00:00:11.187
changed the course of civilization.

3
00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:18.911
Not only is paper used to spread important knowledge and revolutionary ideas, but it also affects people's daily

4
00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:21.573
lives, the modern economy, and the environment.

5
00:00:22.513 --> 00:00:26.436
The word "paper" comes from "papyrus," the plant first used to make paper.

6
00:00:27.272 --> 00:00:33.338
In about 3000 BC, the ancient Egyptians found that laying strips of papyrus at right angles and then

7
00:00:33.378 --> 00:00:36.380
drying them would create thin sheets of cream-colored material.

8
00:00:36.921 --> 00:00:39.103
They recorded important events on these sheets.

9
00:00:39.543 --> 00:00:44.087
Soon, the ancient Greeks adopted this technology, as would the Romans after them.

10
00:00:44.548 --> 00:00:48.211
Paper made from papyrus, however, was difficult to make and expensive.

11
00:00:49.512 --> 00:00:55.408
During the third century BC, the Greeks also began to use bound sheets of parchment (a writing material

12
00:00:55.448 --> 00:01:00.390
made from stretched sheep or goat skin) or vellum (made from the skin of a calf) to write

13
00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:04.991
on. These early books had to be laboriously hand-copied, word by word.

14
00:01:05.531 --> 00:01:08.872
As a result, only the church and the very wealthy could afford them.

15
00:01:09.432 --> 00:01:15.234
Before paper and printing became widespread, very few people had access to information beyond what they were told

16
00:01:15.294 --> 00:01:15.734
at church.

17
00:01:16.474 --> 00:01:22.076
Most scholars agree that a Chinese official invented the method of papermaking that today's processes are based on

18
00:01:22.196 --> 00:01:22.556
in 105 AD.

19
00:01:24.412 --> 00:01:30.897
This technique involved soaking, washing, and then beating rags, tree bark, and other materials into pulp using a

20
00:01:30.937 --> 00:01:31.577
wooden mallet.

21
00:01:32.558 --> 00:01:36.780
The pulp was then laid out on wire mesh and dried to form thin sheets.

22
00:01:38.161 --> 00:01:41.624
Papermaking soon spread from China to other parts of the world.

23
00:01:42.504 --> 00:01:47.247
It was introduced to Korea during the third century and to India through traders traveling the Silk Road

24
00:01:47.288 --> 00:01:48.268
by the eighth century.

25
00:01:49.089 --> 00:01:53.612
Paper eventually reached Europe in the twelfth century through Italian merchants who traded with Arabs.

26
00:01:54.388 --> 00:01:59.709
The knowledge of how to make this material became so valuable that skilled Chinese papermakers were sometimes even

27
00:01:59.749 --> 00:02:02.990
taken captive and forced to make paper for their captors.

28
00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:09.532
An important innovation occurred in the tenth century AD, when a Chinese man, Pi Sheng, invented the first

29
00:02:09.592 --> 00:02:11.393
system of movable type out of clay.

30
00:02:11.893 --> 00:02:16.334
Movable type was a major development because it meant that multiple copies of a book could be printed

31
00:02:16.394 --> 00:02:17.734
easily, cheaply, and quickly.

32
00:02:18.254 --> 00:02:24.156
Then around 1400 AD, King Taejong of Korea ordered the creation of the first metal movable type.

33
00:02:24.972 --> 00:02:31.295
Unfortunately, neither of these inventions was widely used because the eighty thousand characters of the Chinese language made

34
00:02:31.375 --> 00:02:33.095
movable type rather impractical.

35
00:02:33.676 --> 00:02:40.659
Movable type finally gained widespread recognition and use when Johannes Gutenberg invented his version of it in Mainz,

36
00:02:40.699 --> 00:02:42.339
Germany, in 1450.

37
00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:47.942
By the end of that century, printing presses had been set up in more than 250 cities across

38
00:02:48.042 --> 00:02:53.384
Europe. With the use of the printing press, new ideas about culture, religion, and commerce spread quickly throughout

39
00:02:53.484 --> 00:02:59.794
Europe. When printing technology spread to Italy, the Renaissance (a flowering of art, architecture, and literature) was born.

40
00:03:00.674 --> 00:03:06.036
One of the most influential events of the time was the Reformation, the Protestant movement against the Catholic

41
00:03:06.056 --> 00:03:14.338
Church. In 1517, a German religious scholar named Martin Luther started the Reformation when he posted what are

42
00:03:14.419 --> 00:03:20.024
now known as his "95 Theses" on the doors of Castle Church in Wittenburg.

43
00:03:20.044 --> 00:03:26.007
The 95 Theses opposed some of the Catholic Church's practices, most notably the selling of "indulgences," or pardons

44
00:03:26.067 --> 00:03:26.547
for sins.

45
00:03:27.328 --> 00:03:33.631
Others reprinted this document, and soon Luther's views attracted enough followers to weaken the Catholic Church.

46
00:03:34.291 --> 00:03:36.552
This led to the rise of Protestantism.

47
00:03:37.573 --> 00:03:42.335
Thus, printing made it possible for the Reformation to change the role of the church and the religious,

48
00:03:42.395 --> 00:03:43.976
social, and political face of Europe.

49
00:03:45.084 --> 00:03:50.567
In eighteenth-century England, another movement, called the Industrial Revolution, began to change people's lives.

50
00:03:51.007 --> 00:03:54.448
Before then, most people were farmers and lived in the countryside.

51
00:03:54.749 --> 00:04:00.952
During the Industrial Revolution, large sections of the rural population migrated into cities to get jobs in the

52
00:04:00.972 --> 00:04:02.412
many factories being built there.

53
00:04:02.912 --> 00:04:10.276
The Industrial Revolution also changed printing by mechanizing the process of making paper, setting type, printing, and binding.

54
00:04:10.908 --> 00:04:16.132
This meant that books could be mass-produced cheaply, and for the first time, everyone could afford to buy

55
00:04:16.172 --> 00:04:22.916
them. The prevalence of books meant that many people wanted to learn to read, so widespread public education

56
00:04:22.976 --> 00:04:24.818
became more common in the nineteenth century.

57
00:04:25.458 --> 00:04:30.001
As literacy increased, so did demand for magazines, collections of poetry, and novels.

58
00:04:30.702 --> 00:04:35.485
And as the range of available reading materials expanded, so did the modern concept of literature.

59
00:04:36.466 --> 00:04:39.328
Paper also made many contributions to the fine arts.

60
00:04:39.888 --> 00:04:43.712
It became a popular medium for watercolor paintings, sketches, and posters.

61
00:04:44.192 --> 00:04:48.115
The Japanese have even elevated paper-folding to an art form known as "origami."

62
00:04:48.556 --> 00:04:51.358
People began to use paper for other decorative purposes.

63
00:04:51.939 --> 00:04:57.664
For centuries, people in China, Korea, and Japan have been using paper to make windows and decorative screens.

64
00:04:58.544 --> 00:05:03.889
Using paper to create home furnishings is now also popular in many countries all over the world.

65
00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:06.992
Paper has many practical uses as well.

66
00:05:07.264 --> 00:05:10.667
Paper money, for example, has had an enormous impact on society.

67
00:05:11.107 --> 00:05:16.091
Before its introduction, bartering and using coins made of precious metals were the norm.

68
00:05:17.012 --> 00:05:20.815
Paper money, on the other hand, is much easier to carry around and store.

69
00:05:21.315 --> 00:05:27.460
It also has another advantage; it merely represents the value of the wealth, so it is easily replaceable.

70
00:05:28.121 --> 00:05:31.564
Paper money led to the creation of today's credit card–based economies.

71
00:05:32.128 --> 00:05:37.192
The plastic credit cards that move vast amounts of money would not have been possible without the introduction

72
00:05:37.212 --> 00:05:38.293
of paper money first.

73
00:05:38.853 --> 00:05:45.798
Packaging is another area in which paper has become indispensable, particularly in the manufacture of disposable paper products,

74
00:05:45.818 --> 00:05:47.299
such as paper cups and plates.

75
00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:51.843
But the availability of paper goods has led to overconsumption of paper products.

76
00:05:52.583 --> 00:05:59.484
For instance, North Americans now consume approximately three hundred kilograms of paper per person each year, compared to

77
00:05:59.504 --> 00:06:02.045
the world average of about fifty kilograms per person.

78
00:06:02.906 --> 00:06:08.548
This is another way that paper has changed our lives—the demand for large quantities of paper-based products is

79
00:06:08.588 --> 00:06:10.229
now causing environmental damage.

80
00:06:10.849 --> 00:06:17.932
A proliferation of paper-based advertisements, office documents, and junk mail has resulted in ever-increasing amounts of paper being

81
00:06:17.972 --> 00:06:23.694
thrown away, even while trees are continually being cut, leading to deforestation and global warming.

82
00:06:24.254 --> 00:06:27.816
On average, one tree produces about eighty thousand sheets of paper.

83
00:06:28.332 --> 00:06:33.453
To supply the world's paper needs for one year, about 786 million trees are cut down.

84
00:06:33.934 --> 00:06:39.675
When paper was invented almost two thousand years ago, its inventor could not have imagined the changes that

85
00:06:39.695 --> 00:06:40.155
it would bring.

86
00:06:40.735 --> 00:06:44.136
It is difficult to imagine a modern world without this valuable material.

87
00:06:44.616 --> 00:06:49.318
It is no wonder that the Chinese consider paper to be one of their culture's foremost inventions.

88
00:06:49.838 --> 00:06:53.559
Indeed, it is one of the foremost inventions of human history.
